nnot do that  however this one the next gen sequencing  needs a baseline sample all the time  so to compare it to through the  progression  while flow cytometry doesn't which is  actually a plus  for flow cytometry technically  the next generation sequencing is  is an available service commercially  however it's not available in most  hospitals and it's not available in most  academic  institutions so you need to send that  out to outsource it to companies  to do that while flow cytometry is  wide available everywhere almost in  every hematology clinical or research  lab  in universities in hospitals and  everywhere  so that's a plus for flow cytometry as  of complexity  you know the machines and the  bioinformatics needed for next gene  sequencing is much more complicated and  expensive  for flow cytometry it's pretty simple  all what you need  is standard software and for the  turnaround which sometimes very  important especially in relapsed  patients  for next gen sequencing we need about  two weeks  while the flow cytometry you get the  results in three four hours  that's again a huge advantage for flow  cytometry  and the cost the cost per sample for  an ngs is about a thousand five hundred  dollars per sample  while for flow it's three hundred  dollars so there is kind of a clear  advantage  for flow cytometry over next-gen  sequencing  a lot of people would agree with me some  people will not  but at least from technical point of  view  flow cytometry is easier and more  available  so currently there are three markers  to identify myeloma cells by flow  cytometry  and i'll start from the bottom up  so we start with cd45 which is a pan  leukocyte marker it just identifies all  white blood cells  cd45 is a surface adhesion molecule  and then we have to complex it to  add another layer of specificity we look  at cd38 which is a pan  lymphocyte marker which is expressed on  b  cells these cells in case and some other  melody cells  but it's now a little more focused  towards the myeloma and it's generally  highly expressed in myeloma too  and the third marker which is the gold  standard for detecting myeloma currently  is cd138 which is also another adhesion  molecule  but it's specific very specific for  plasma cells  so if you notice these are all  cell adhesion molecules and  they are mediating the interaction  between multiple myeloma and the tumor  microenvironment  and we have been studying the cell cell  adhesion  between myeloma and its tumor micro  environment intensively over the last 10  years  we now understand the cellular processes  behind that  and the cell signaling and if  if we will have to go deeper in that i'm  more than happy to do so later  but it's it's not the main focus of this  uh presentation  but what i wanted to emphasize that this  interaction  is very important for the development  progression and and dissemination of  multiple myeloma  and now we in our lab we have  characterized that very deeply and we  understand it very well  and what's the role of each of these  molecules  in the process of progression  one important feature of the multiple  myeloma tumor micro environment  is hypoxia we found that  the more progressed is the tumor  there is more tumor hypoxia hypoxia is  the lack of oxygen  and it happens because the progression  rate of myeloma  out rates the progress the development  of  blood vessels so we will have more tumor  than what the body could supply blood  vessels to it  so the tumors become hypoxic now  what what why is that important we found  that actually this changes dramatically  the properties of the multiple myeloma  cell  they become much more aggressive they  become much more metastatic  they start going to new places  through aggress from the primary side  to the blood and homing to new sites  in addition it makes it make way much  more drug resistant  the hypoxic cells are less going cell  cycle  so they become more resistant to  chemotherapy  and they display st