n for cd38  we know we will be picking now all  myeloma cells  the cd138 positive and the cd3138  negative which are in the uh black  uh oval here but we will also be picking  others like t cells b cells  and k cells etc so we will add  antibodies to stain these specifically  and exclude them from the total group so  we add cd3  to remove t cells we add cd19 to take  out  b cells to uh take in case we add cd16  and etc so we have a line of  exclusion markers what we call cd3 cd 14  cd 16 19 and 123  this will ensure when we add them  to the mix we will ensure that we can  exclude  all the cells which are not myeloma but  still cd  38 positive now we put  all these exclusion marker in one  channel in the flow cytometry  for example in the 5c channel but it  doesn't have to be  and we put the 38 which is the positive  marker  on another channel like the apc but we  also it doesn't also have to be it can  be any other color  so the idea here is that the multiple  myeloma cell  will be 38 positive but negative for  for all the others regardless if  it's 138 positive or 138  negative and we will i'll show you in a  minute  how this will actually start picking  more myeloma cells which the classic  gold standard marker cd138  will not fit what we started with here  was that we combined  normal bone marrow cells that they don't  have myeloma  we combine these with a myeloma cell  line  we added exactly 10 percent cells into  the normal  bone marrow cells and once we added  normal or neuromoxic myeloma cells or we  added myeloma cells which were  put in hypoxia before the combination  then we tried to find them back either  with cd138 which is the cold standard  or with cd 38 alone without the  exclusion or with the new method  so when we try to find with 138  we get in the normoxic cells  we get about seven percent out of the  ten  we get less than five percent if the  cells were hypoxic  which actually means that half of the  cells that we put  if they would be hypoxic we wouldn't  even detect them  using the gold standard model  if we just use cd 38 alone we are  picking now 25  instead of 10 because we are picking  other cells  not only myeloma but when we use the new  method  that we just defined we pick back  exactly 10 which is what we put to start  with  so we took that and we took it a little  further  in vivo and  we uh put myeloma cells gfp labeled  green fluorescence protein label so we  could track them  and we put them in mice in skid mice  in different amounts and we let them  grow for almost three weeks  so we got mice with different levels  of myeloma involvement in their bone  marrow  we take the bone marrow out just like we  do in patients we take a bone marrow  biopsy or a bone marrow aspirate  so we isolated bone marrow from these  mice  and we we compared how much we can  detect myeloma cells  in the marrow using gfp  which is actually an artificial color  that we put  so every cell that we that's gfp is  by definition myeloma then we try to see  if we could find these using the gold  standard marker and as you see  in the right part despite that the  amount of gfp  slash myeloma cells was increasing  dramatically  out up to 65 percent in the in the bone  marrow  the cd 138 could barely detect  10 so this is a huge false negative  while if we look at the new method we  picked  exactly the same amount that the gfp  found this gives us another layer of  confidence  that now we are not missing any myeloma  cell  but even more importantly we are not  picking  any false positive so we took that  and we wanted to validate another layer  in patients so we took patient bone  marrow aspirates  and we looked first of all at cd 138  positive and negative we just took the  138  negative portion which is supposed to be  no myeloma cells according to the  classic definition  we detected with the new method  so we isolated the apc positive  fitsy negative as we defined it before  and we looked at the clonality so  myeloma cells