hem clinically  they're they stay there  they're almost undetected  and they induce remission after  remission they induce  relapse so how that works  what's the concept so if we treat and we  bring  the the tumor burden let's say  10 times lower so we will have a  kind of short period of remission if we  make the tumor burden a hundred times  lower  we will have a longer emission and so on  until we get almost to no cells  which will will say no remission  that's probably theoretical most of the  time we get to like  10 100 cells if we are really lucky  then we have long periods of remission  so that these minimum amounts of cells  are very hard to detect you know why  because  as i said before most likely these cells  are drug resistant are the very hypoxic  ones  which are actually cd138  negative so  it's now known that the definition of  mrd will change how we treat because  it's not  enough to just reduce the size of the  tumor  we want to bring it to a level that's  even these minimal residual disease will  be gone  however how to detect these with  what's the optimal technology is exactly  the question  so we suggested using this flow  cytometry technique that we developed  to detect minimal residual disease  because it's it's again  independent of cd138 and we tried  to find a connection between the amount  of cells that we found  in the bone marrow using our technology  with the time  takes the patient to relapse  so we took a group of patients  after treatment they were defined cured  they were defined cr and we looked  into them with their bone marrow using  the new technology or using  cd 138 the standard uh model  so with cd 138 they all looked  pretty pretty clean less than half  percent if you look at the left  box and there is no difference  we couldn't really find any difference  in um the amount of cd 138 cells  in patients who relapsed fast less than  24  months or in patients who relapsed  slow or longer but when we look  at the bone marrow with