nts and we  actually did find a lot of circulating  cells  in the patients while with cd138 we  found  again nothing less than 0.5  so we are we tried also to  look at if we can predict uh  progression-free survival  and see if that correlates we did find a  correlation  not as strong statistically as before  but it's statistically significant  it's highly statistical significance so  in patients  who had a fast relapse  we found a high amount of circulating  myeloma cells  while the ones who relapsed later  we found lower amounts of the  circulating cells  so just in the end to compare all the  three  uh technologies that we have available  right now  so we have next-gen sequencing it's  um quantitative and highly quantitative  for detection of mrd  it is while the cd138  is not that good for detection of 138  our new markers are highly quantitative  prediction of a progression-free  survival  it is right with next-gen sequencing  not with flaw cytometry based on cd138  but yes for our markers  time of analysis for next-gen sequencing  as we said it's one to two weeks  in our in all in both flow techniques  it's two to three hours  availability of the service for next-gen  sequencing we need to outsource  the flow is in every lab and  sample preparation no primers  so we need primers and we need reference  samples for next-gen sequencing  no such requirements are needed for flow  detection of circulating tumor cells  it has never been shown yet by next  generation sequencing i'm sure  that at some point people will start  exploring that  it's poor in uh cd 138 based flow  we can detect it with our new technology  using flow cytometry  so that's a huge advantage and the cost  is pretty low  for both the flow techniques and the  cd138 so i think the future  is going to be something close to the  technology that we developed  and at that point i will uh thank you  for uh listening and i uh  would like to open it for questions  thank you professor azab for that  outstanding presentation  we will now move into the live q a  portion of our webinar  now as a reminder to our audience please  submit your questions via the q a box  okay professor azab here's our first  question  although cd 138 is considered the gold  standard marker for myeloma  as you mentioned and others have also  shown that it is shed  from the cell surface in some mm  populations  can you elaborate more on what the mm  cells lose  their cd138 surface expression  sure um i mentioned before  uh cd 138 is an adhesion molecule  it interacts with integrands uh to uh  promote cell cell adhesion but we found  that the expression of this surface  marker is actually reduced  due to many stresses and  probably tumor hypoxia is the most  important one of them but it's not the  only one  for example treatment with chemotherapy  would even reduce it  even lower so if you think about these  myeloma patients that were heavily  treated with chemotherapy  and the cells are hypoxic and drug  resistant and have cd 138 low  now we treat them with more chemotherapy  and they even  lose even more the cd-138 expression  therefore now these cells will become  almost  unseen in the patients so  we will observe their occurrence  because of these cells actually in most  of 90 of the patients because we miss  these cells  in more than 90 percent of the patients  thank you our next question there have  been several attempts to develop  cd 138 independent markers for myeloma  how is this new method different  right yeah i i agree you know the uh  especially  uh there is uh um  a lot of research on that especially  coming from europe  and um the methods that are now used  uh are really complex they call them  as of their complexity they call them  next-gen  uh fluorocytometry because uh it's based  on  uh 9 to 8 color flow  which makes it technically very complex  and hard  you will need special machines to do  that special flow cytometry machines  and you will need very highly skilled  techni